  {
    "episode_id": 77803,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/7/77803.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 33,
    "turn_text": "He has no choice. I mean, look, normally in a debt limit fight, the voters tend to blame Congress. Obama had a double-digit advantage over Republicans in 2011 when he had his debt limit fight with them. And he still agreed to massive spending cuts in the Budget Control Act. Right now, according to our Fox News poll, 47% would blame Biden. If there was a default, only 44% would blame the GOP. 57% of Americans support the position of spending cuts in exchange for debt limit increase. Only 27% support Biden, saying that he wanted a clean debt limit increase. And Biden's numbers on the economy are underwater. So the Republicans have a lot of leverage right now, especially because, as you say, they passed their bill. They don't have that much leverage. Because if Biden comes to the table and makes some sort of reasonable deal and they turn it down or McCarthy cuts a deal and the House Republicans don't go along with it, then they can get the blame for it. And neither side wants to be blamed for it. Biden doesn't want more blame for the bad economy and Republicans don't want to give him a pretext to shift the blame for the bad economy.",
